# Every variable the compose stack (deploy/compose.yaml) reads, with its default.
# Create deploy/.env from this file with `scripts/setup-env.sh`: it fills in random tokens,
# makes the file readable by its owner only, and never overwrites an existing one. Then
# fill in the rest. deploy/.env is never committed.
# After changing deploy/.env, run `docker compose -f deploy/compose.yaml up -d --wait`:
# it recreates the services whose environment changed (`restart` keeps the old values).
#
# Lines starting with "# NAME=" show a default; remove the "# " to change it.

# Name of this installation: prefixes the stack's containers, volumes and networks. Change it
# (with TEND_PORT and TEND_ENGINE_UI_PORT) to run a second stack on the same Docker engine.
# COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME=tendworks
# Port of the control plane API, web UI and platform MCP server, published on 127.0.0.1 only.
# TEND_PORT=8080
# Port of the workflow engine's web UI, published on 127.0.0.1 only and only with
# `--profile debug` (it has no login).
# TEND_ENGINE_UI_PORT=8233
# Configuration directory (workflows, agent profiles, ...), relative to deploy/.
# TEND_CONFIG_PATH=../examples/config
# Workspace root: directory workspaces (kind Workspace with `directory: { path }`) must lie
# strictly under it, also after resolving symbolic links and `..`; anything else is refused
# at startup and before each session. An absolute host path, e.g. /srv/tendworks/workspaces.
# The stack mounts it read-only into server and worker at the same path; sandboxes get only
# their workspace's directory, read-write unless a session may only read. Unset: no
# directory workspaces. One root only: the stack mounts the value as one directory
# (docs/operator-guide.md, "Directory workspaces").
# TEND_WORKSPACE_ROOTS=

# Heartbeat: while the platform is healthy, the server calls this URL (GET, about every
# five minutes) so an external push monitor (healthchecks.io, Uptime Kuma) can alarm when
# the pings stop. Unset: no heartbeat (docs/operator-guide.md, "Heartbeat").
# TEND_HEARTBEAT_URL=

# Store password: scripts/setup-env.sh generates one; server and worker refuse to start
# without it. The store takes it only when it creates its data; to change it later, change
# it in the store first (docs/operator-guide.md, "Rotation").
TEND_POSTGRES_PASSWORD=

# Residual risk: sandboxes still reach the API's published port where the Docker host
# forwards it (Docker Desktop); until the egress proxy, these tokens are the guard, not
# the network. No sandbox gets the API token; only sessions of profiles that list the
# platform MCP server get the read-only token, and only their sandboxes reach the
# MCP-only gateway.
#
# API token: every operation but GET /status needs a bearer token, and operations that
# change state (start a run, decide an approval request) need this one; the tend
# CLI and the smoke scripts read it from TEND_API_TOKEN too.
# At least 16 characters; scripts/setup-env.sh generates one (or `openssl rand -hex 32`).
# The server refuses to start without it.
TEND_API_TOKEN=
# Read-only token: accepted for reading only, refused (403) by operations that change
# state; for clients that should only read, e.g. the platform MCP server of chat agents.
# At least 16 characters and different from TEND_API_TOKEN; scripts/setup-env.sh generates
# one. The server refuses to start without it. It is never an agent secret: agent profiles
# cannot reference it.
TEND_API_READ_TOKEN=
# Address of the platform MCP server as sandboxes reach it. By default the stack's
# MCP-only gateway, which forwards nothing but /mcp to the server and which only sandboxes
# of profiles that list the platform MCP server reach, on Linux and Docker Desktop alike.
# Change it only if you put your own proxy in its place.
# TEND_PLATFORM_MCP_URL=http://mcp-gateway:8080/mcp

# Harness credentials for agent profiles with harness claude-code.
# TEND_CLAUDE_CODE_CREDENTIAL selects which one goes into the sandbox: oauth-token
# (subscription token from `claude setup-token`; single-operator use only under
# Anthropic's terms) or api-key. Only the selected variable is used, never both.
TEND_CLAUDE_CODE_CREDENTIAL=
CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN=
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# Optional alternative model endpoint, e.g. a gateway
ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=

# Agent secrets: every TENDWORKS_AGENT_* variable in this file can be referenced by
# workspaces (credentials: `secretRef: { name: TENDWORKS_AGENT_<NAME> }`) and agent
# profiles (spec.env: `NAME: { secretRef: { name: TENDWORKS_AGENT_<NAME> } }`) and goes
# into the sandboxes that use them. To add one, add a line TENDWORKS_AGENT_<NAME>=<value>
# here and `up -d` again; deploy/compose.yaml needs no change, and the worker needs no
# restart. Platform secrets (TENDWORKS_PLATFORM_*) never reach the worker or a sandbox.

# The two GitHub tokens, both fine-grained:
# - Agent token, one per workspace (here the starter's workspace playground,
#   examples/config/workspaces/playground.yaml): its credential for git, also seen by the
#   session as GH_TOKEN. That repository only, contents and pull requests read/write. It
#   goes into the sandboxes of that workspace's tasks. Another workspace gets its own
#   TENDWORKS_AGENT_<NAME>_TOKEN line.
# - Issues token: the github-issues trigger (`token: { secretRef: { name:
#   TENDWORKS_PLATFORM_GITHUB_TOKEN } }` in a trigger binding) reads issues with it. The
#   repositories of the bindings' workspaces, issues read-only. Server only, never in a
#   sandbox.
# Which permissions each token gets, what that prevents and what it does not:
# docs/github-agent-access.md; check the agent token with
# scripts/check-github-agent-access.sh --repo OWNER/NAME --env-file deploy/.env
TENDWORKS_AGENT_PLAYGROUND_TOKEN=
TENDWORKS_PLATFORM_GITHUB_TOKEN=

# Pushover: notification channels with `integration: pushover` reference the application's
# API token and the user (or group) key as
#  `pushover: { token: { secretRef: { name: TENDWORKS_PLATFORM_PUSHOVER_TOKEN } },
#              user: { secretRef: { name: TENDWORKS_PLATFORM_PUSHOVER_USER } } }`.
# Platform secrets: only the server holds them, never a sandbox (ADR 0025). Create an
# application at https://pushover.net/apps/build; the user key is on your Pushover dashboard.
# The starter's channel template: examples/config/notifications/phone.yaml.example
# (docs/operator-guide.md, "Pushover on your phone").
TENDWORKS_PLATFORM_PUSHOVER_TOKEN=
TENDWORKS_PLATFORM_PUSHOVER_USER=
